---
import SectionHeading from "./SectionHeading.astro";

// DRAFT copy — a coached first pass on Kha's career story. Keep editing
// until every sentence sounds like something he'd actually say out loud.
//
// GO-LIVE GATE (as of 2026-08-01) — see the matching note on SUMMARY in
// site.config.mjs. The closing paragraph below claims Kha is maintaining a
// real dbt warehouse on BigQuery. As of this date that's not true yet — zero
// hands-on dbt/BigQuery work has happened, only learning. This is
// deliberate target-state copy: don't deploy (merge to `main`) until the
// warehouse actually exists to match what's written here.
const STORY = [
  "I graduated in Mechatronics Engineering from Ho Chi Minh City University of Technology, then joined Keppel Land Vietnam's Management Associate program — a rotation built for future business leaders, not for data people.",
  "Coordinating a large residential handover, I built Excel trackers just to make sense of what was happening — which contractors were behind, what the team needed to look at that week. I wasn't chasing data as a career. I just liked that a formula could tell me the truth faster than a meeting could — the spark, though I didn't notice it at the time.",
  "That spark followed me to KONE, where I went from reading a spreadsheet to building the dashboards an entire operations team relied on. Along the way I taught myself SQL and Python simply because I wanted to understand more — before I even had a problem that needed them.",
  "The same curiosity brought me to Prudential, where I now build the systems a life insurer's agency network trusts for real decisions — and it's what pulled me toward the next layer down. Right now that's a dbt warehouse on BigQuery, modeling insurance policy and claims data with staging-to-mart layering, tests, and lineage I have to maintain myself.",
  "Dashboards taught me what a business needs to see. Analytics engineering is how I make the layer underneath trustworthy enough to serve it. I don't think that curiosity burns out — I think it's what keeps taking me further.",
];
---

<section class="section" id="about">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="about-inner">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Background" title="About" />
      <div class="about-story">
        {STORY.map((paragraph) => <p data-reveal>{paragraph}</p>)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Constrained inside the standard container rather than swapping to
     .container-narrow, so the section's left edge lines up with every other
     section on the page instead of jumping inward. */
  .about-inner {
    max-width: 44rem;
  }

  .about-story {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .about-story p {
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--color-secondary);
  }

  /* The closing pair is the positioning statement — lift it out of the
     body-copy grey so it lands as a conclusion rather than trailing off. */
  .about-story p:last-child {
    color: var(--color-foreground);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
</style>
